{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q003_actin_like_atpase_divergence","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08 (stable), actin/HSP70, hexokinase, and glycerol kinase are all placed in fold c.55 (Ribonuclease H-like motif) and superfamily c.55.1 (Actin-like ATPase domain). They sit in different families: c.55.1.1 Actin/HSP70 (sunid 53068), c.55.1.3 Hexokinase (sunid 53083), and c.55.1.4 Glycerol kinase (sunid 53089). The superfamily's children list also includes c.55.1.2 Acetokinase-like, c.55.1.5 BadF/BadG/BcrA/BcrD-like, and c.55.1.6 ATPase domain of dehydratase reactivase alpha subunit.

What this permits: SCOPe's superfamily level is the deepest homology-bearing assertion here. Because the three families share superfamily c.55.1, SCOPe supports probable common ancestry of this actin-like ATPase core at the superfamily level. It does not support a close (family-level) sequence relationship, since the families differ. The superfamily description itself names an ATPase domain, but SCOPe does not separately annotate each protein's catalytic residues or reaction.

What it does not permit: SCOPe does not establish shared cellular role, substrate, reaction, ligand site, oligomeric state, or an interchangeable structural template. Divergent functions (cytoskeletal actin/HSP70 chaperones, hexose phosphorylation by hexokinase, glycerol phosphorylation by glycerol kinase) are consistent with superfamily-level ancestry plus functional divergence, but that interpretation needs external evidence such as active-site motif correspondence, a global structural alignment with topology, or biochemical data. None of these was retrieved here. The retrieved data also do not show a BLAST or FATCAT comparison between these families. Hexokinase was verified only at family and protein-node level; I did not retrieve a hexokinase domain record. The glycerol kinase domain d1r59o1 was verified through its parents endpoint. Homology records for c.55.1, c.55.1.1, c.55.1.3, and c.55.1.4 show homology_implied_by_level=true with no warnings. The superfamily annotation carries only the comment 'duplication contains two domains of this fold'. The absence of warnings is not independent proof of homology. The stable 2.08 release was used; the latest periodic update (2.08-2026-08-16) was not examined.","evidence_used":["SKILL.md (scop-homology/SKILL.md)","references/api-usage.md (scop-homology/references/api-usage.md)","references/biological-interpretation.md (scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md)","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=actin&limit=5","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=hexokinase&limit=5","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=glycerol+kinase&limit=5","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53075","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53075/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/104926/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53083","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53083/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53089/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53067/children?limit=100","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53067/homolog